Initiative Framework: 

The first step of this process will be to quickly learn what people do today and where it breaks. 

Watch 2–3 people do a real task (15 minutes each) 

Ask 5 questions: where do you store files, how do you name them, how do you share changes, what breaks most often, what do you wish existed 

Collect 3 real examples of project folders (good and messy) 

List the top 5 failure points (ex: overwritten scripts, missing “how to run,” unclear owner) 

Confirm what tools are allowed/available (shared drive, Git, docs, permissions) 

Hypothesis: My hypothesis is that one standard project folder structure + naming rules + a short “handoff checklist” will reduce confusion and prevent most collaboration issues on shared HAAG systems. 

Experiment:  

To experiment with this, I will pilot the workflow with one active team for 2 weeks. 

Set up one “model” project folder using the standard structure 

Require one simple rule: “No changes to shared scripts without updating the README” 

Use the handoff checklist when someone passes work to another person 

Track: number of overwrite/conflict incidents, time spent finding files, and how often people ask for help locating things 

Report & Iterate 

With the pilot complete, I will do a short feedback check and update the procedure. 

15-minute debrief with the team: what helped, what was annoying, what was ignored 

Update the procedure to be “must-do” (3–5 rules) vs “nice-to-have” 

Final artifact: a one-page procedure + example folder screenshot + README and handoff template 
